  "contentHtml": "<div class=\"section\"><h2 id=\"overview\">Overview</h2><p>The <strong>Boeing 787-9 Dreamliner</strong> is United's core long-haul international aircraft and the most numerous 787 in the fleet, with <strong>48 jets</strong> flying from the airline's largest hubs. United is <strong>Boeing's largest 787 customer</strong>, and the 787-9 anchors a historic Dreamliner expansion: its December 2022 order for 100 Dreamliners was the largest 787 order in Boeing history, with deliveries running into the early 2030s as the type replaces aging Boeing 767s and 777-200s.</p><p>The newest 787-9s are also United's most premium jets — they introduce the <a href=\"#polaris-studio\">Polaris Studio</a> suite and a premium-heavy \"Elevated\" cabin, making this the flagship of United's widebody fleet.</p><div class=\"highlight-box\"><strong>Key facts:</strong> 48 aircraft · 257 seats (standard) · 48 Polaris (1-2-1) · GE GEnx-1B engines · Composite fuselage · Deliveries 2014–2026 · Exclusive home of Polaris Studio</div></div><div class=\"section\"><h2 id=\"seat-config\">Seat Configuration</h2><p>Most of United's 787-9 fleet flies the <strong>standard 257-seat layout</strong> below, and it remains the majority configuration today. The newest deliveries use the premium-heavy <a href=\"#polaris-studio\">\"Elevated\"</a> layout shown beneath it.</p><h3>Standard layout — 257 seats</h3><table class=\"config-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cabin</th><th>Seats</th><th>Layout</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"cabin-tag cabin-polaris\">Polaris Business</span></td><td>48</td><td>1-2-1</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"cabin-tag cabin-pp\">Premium Plus</span></td><td>21</td><td>2-3-2</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"cabin-tag cabin-ep\">Economy Plus</span></td><td>39</td><td>3-3-3</td></tr><tr><td>Economy</td><td>149</td><td>3-3-3</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>\"Elevated\" layout — 222 seats (newest jets)</h3><p>United's latest 787-9s trade some economy space for a far larger front cabin: <strong>99 premium seats</strong> (64 Polaris plus 35 Premium Plus) — the <strong>highest premium ratio of any U.S.-carrier widebody</strong>. Economy Plus remains a section within Economy.</p><table class=\"config-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cabin</th><th>Seats</th><th>Layout</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"cabin-tag cabin-polaris\">Polaris Business</span> <span style=\"color:var(--ua-muted)\">(incl. 8 Polaris Studio)</span></td><td>64</td><td>1-2-1</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"cabin-tag cabin-pp\">Premium Plus</span></td><td>35</td><td>2-3-2</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"cabin-tag cabin-ep\">Economy Plus</span></td><td>33</td><td>3-3-3</td></tr><tr><td>Economy</td><td>90</td><td>3-3-3</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"section\"><h2 id=\"polaris-studio\">Polaris Studio &amp; the \"Elevated\" 787-9</h2><p><strong>Polaris Studio</strong> is United's new ultra-premium suite, and it is <strong>exclusive to the 787-9</strong> — no other aircraft type carries it. Each Elevated 787-9 has <strong>8 Polaris Studio suites</strong> at the front of the Polaris cabin.</p><p>Every Studio suite is roughly <strong>25% larger than a standard Polaris seat</strong> and adds a <strong>sliding privacy door</strong>, a <strong>27-inch 4K OLED touchscreen</strong>, Bluetooth audio, wireless charging, a <strong>companion ottoman</strong> for dining with a guest, and <strong>Saks Fifth Avenue bedding</strong>. It sells for roughly a <strong>$499 upsell</strong> over a standard Polaris seat.</p><p>Polaris Studio entered service on <strong>April 22, 2026</strong> (San Francisco–Singapore), followed by San Francisco–London Heathrow from April 30. United expects to fly about <strong>20 Elevated 787-9s in 2026</strong>, growing to <strong>47 by 2028</strong>. Until then, the standard 257-seat 787-9 remains the majority of the fleet.</p><div class=\"highlight-box\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> with 99 premium seats, the Elevated 787-9 carries the highest premium-seat ratio of any widebody flown by a U.S. carrier — a deliberate bet on premium long-haul demand.</div></div><div class=\"section\"><h2 id=\"wifi\">WiFi &amp; In-Flight Entertainment</h2><p>Every 787-9 currently flies with <strong>Ku-band satellite WiFi</strong>, seat-back AVOD plus personal-device entertainment, and <strong>110V AC power</strong> at every seat. The Dreamliner cabin adds larger dimmable windows, higher humidity, and a lower cabin altitude to reduce jet lag on ultra-long-haul flights.</p><p>United began rolling out <strong>free Starlink WiFi</strong> across its widebody fleet in June 2026 — the first widebody Starlink flight was a Boeing 777 on June 22, 2026 — and aims to equip the entire widebody fleet by summer 2027, so 787-9 retrofits are on the way. Starlink is <strong>free for MileagePlus members</strong>.</p></div><div class=\"section\"><h2 id=\"routes\">Routes &amp; Hubs</h2><ul><li><a href=\"/hubs/ewr\">Newark (EWR)</a> — Europe, Middle East, India</li><li><a href=\"/hubs/sfo\">San Francisco (SFO)</a> — Asia-Pacific and Australia; launch base for the Polaris Studio \"Elevated\" jets (SFO–Singapore, SFO–London Heathrow)</li><li><a href=\"/hubs/lax\">Los Angeles (LAX)</a> — Australia and Asia</li><li><a href=\"/hubs/iah\">Houston (IAH)</a> — South America and Europe</li></ul><p>With a range of roughly 7,600 nautical miles, the 787-9 reaches nearly all of United's long-haul network; see the <a href=\"#specs\">Specifications</a> table for full figures.</p><h3>Related Dreamliners</h3><p><a href=\"/fleet/787-8-dreamliner\">787-8</a> (12) is the smaller sibling. <a href=\"/fleet/787-10-dreamliner\">787-10</a> (21) is the stretched, highest-capacity variant.</p></div>"
